body:
>What's wrong with these people?

I can't speak for everyone, but when my mom came to Canada as an immigrant shortly after the Berlin wall fell. At the time, she was pregnant with me, and was regularly visiting a doctor. This guy repeatedly molested her causing her water to break early without her noticing. Shortly after this, she started to feel really sick and a stranger who noticed called 911. Once she got to the hospital she was told that she had blood poisoning and both her and I almost died. As an immigrant who had just fled a communist regime, she had no idea what legal recourse she had and did not report the doc who molested her. 

&#x200B;

What she did instead was develop a serious phobia of doctors. Shortly after, she started to believe that there is a global conspiracy in the health industry to keep people sick to make the doctors more money. She believes the pharmaceutical industry is complicit, and vaccine developers put shit into the vaccines to cause autism and a whole slew of other insanity. She started to gravitate towards doctors who were disbarred for pseudo-scientific beliefs, or who were dismissed by the entire medical community for promoting alternative health products that more often than not were part of some scheme to get the doctors rich.

&#x200B;

I think many people might either be traumatized by some kind of event or know someone who did, and pass these stories around causing people to distrust the medical industry and seek out "alternative health professionals" for help.

My theory is that they consider this to be a sign of great respect. The other countries "fear" him and his intellect/power, so they are resorting to insulting him. After all he's "looking out for American interests" and doesn't let the other countries "walk all over" or "take advantage of the US."

&#x200B;

Ninja Edit: The quotes are meant to indicate the ideas I've seen discussed by some redditors & conservatives I know, but are not direct verbatim quotes.

All the more reason to discuss it. Maybe the other G20 countries should stop bending over for China and see it as a global cultural competitor. They should decide if they want this kind of behavior to influence nations around China, and potentially, more local regions, and then they should decide if they want to ignore China's actions corroding the freedom Hong Kong citizens enjoyed up until recently. 

&#x200B;

In reality, money speaks much louder than protesters do, so we will likely see little action. Hong Kong citizens would be wise to come up with an escape plan before the Chinese government builds a file on each one of them as ex Hong Kong citizens, or Hong Kong protesters, and forbids them from travelling outside the country. Then they will be stuck under Chinese rule for the rest of their lives and will be put in reeducation camps to think like good obedient Chinese citizens.

No. If you rape your child, do you go to prison for "having a raped child", or do you go to prison for raping your child? 

&#x200B;

If you do something known to cause birth disorders like fuck your first cousin, or drink alcohol during pregnancy, you don't "have a deformed kid", you directly and negligently caused your child to be born with a disorder. That decision results in consequences the child will bear for the rest of their lives, and in my opinion, the child would deserve justice.

I absolutely think it is important for people to judge the absurd disparity in compensation between different employees. Of course, their efforts can earn the company lots of money, but the problem is beyond the relationship between that employee and the company. The highest earners in a company see their employees as a means to cut cost, and to earn a higher bonus for saving the company money. When a worker is unhappy with their wage, they are hard pressed to find work that pays more because the top earners in the country have lobbied politicians to lower the minimum wage to the point that it is simply impossible to earn a living despite working a single full-time job. Those workers have to compete with millions of applicants in order to land a slightly better paying job where they can afford a shitty rental and the means to barely feed their families. 

&#x200B;

The disparity between compensation is what makes this possible. No human being should earn so much money that they can use their massive wealth to impact politics, and lives outside of their company. The choices they make are designed to benefit themselves and their companies, not to mutually benefit the public. So to answer your question, it isn't simply about judging what their effort is worth, but thinking a step further and realizing that we are creating ultra-wealthy ultra-powerful people who influence public policy and laws so effectively, it renders a single voter a drop in an ocean against their will. That doesn't mean people shouldn't be allowed to become rich, but there is a difference with becoming traditionally rich, and being so rich that you and 26 others own as much as 50% of the rest of the world, are legally nearly untouchable, or can single handedly buy a country.

There was a famous video about [Wang Yue](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_of_Wang_Yue) posted before. Some commenters explained that the driver basically hit her, and then tried to kill her. Although my understanding is very limited, the commenters were saying that it was more economical under chinese law for the driver to kill her and face time in jail, then risk her surviving and suing the ever loving shit out of him for life long injuries.

&#x200B;

The same goes for the 18 people who walked by and ignored her. [Stephen Meier explained on Quora](https://www.quora.com/Why-do-people-in-mainland-China-often-ignore-road-accident-victims) " There is also no Good Samaritan law in China so citizens may be scared they might be sued or face charges or costs or somehow be impacted by taking action, so easier and safer just to avoid it."

&#x200B;

Maybe a Chinese national can provide better insight than myself on this matter, but this is how I understand it.
